</table></div>
<h3>Daily Tracking Checklist:</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Log Your Scores:</strong> Keep a notebook or mobile spreadsheet by your oche. Track your Bob's 27 finish score and your 100-Dart score once per week.</li>
<li><strong>Stop on Good Form:</strong> When your 20 minutes expire, finish on a clean double or a treble 20 rather than walking away after a missed dart. Ending on a positive neural pathway reinforces confidence.</li>
<li><strong>Re-Check Stance &amp; Balance:</strong> If your grouping suddenly widens during the scoring phase, pause for ten seconds, reset your front foot against the oche, and verify your elbow height before resuming.</li>
</ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-card faq-section">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>How long should you practice darts every day?</h3>
            <p>For most amateur and league players, <strong>20 to 45 minutes of deliberate practice</strong> per day is far more effective than a two-hour unstructured session once a week. Deliberate practice requires intense mental concentration; past 45 minutes, mental fatigue sets in, leading to lazy follow-throughs and bad mechanical habits.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Is it better to practice scoring or doubles first?</h3>
            <p>Beginners should spend approximately 60% of their practice time on board grouping and scoring mechanics to establish a repeatable arm action. Once you can reliably hit fat singles and average 45+ in 501, shift your focus so that <strong>at least 50% to 60% of your practice time is dedicated to doubles and checkouts</strong>. As the old darts axiom states: scoring gets you to the board, but doubles win the cash.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>Why do I score high in practice but miss everything in league matches?</h3>
            <p>In solo practice, you throw without consequences. If you miss treble 20, you instantly pull another dart from your hand and correct your line. In a live match, your opponent throws between your visits, introducing pressure, pauses, and adrenaline. To bridge this gap, replace open throwing with pressure routines like <strong>Bob's 27</strong> and <strong>The 50-Up/Down Ladder</strong>, where missed darts carry an immediate penalty.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>What is the most effective double to practice for beginners?</h3>
            <p>Focus on the "Rule of Halves" starting on <strong>Double 20 (Tops)</strong> and <strong>Double 16</strong>. If you miss Double 20 inside into single 20, you have an even number left (Double 10). If you miss Double 10 inside, you leave Double 5. Similarly, Double 16 breaks down smoothly into Double 8, Double 4, Double 2, and Double 1. Mastering Double 20 and Double 16 gives you safety margins on match-winning visits.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-item">
            <h3>How do I track my darts practice progress over time?</h3>
            <p>Pick two measurable benchmark drills—such as <strong>The 100-Dart Treble 20 Challenge</strong> and <strong>Bob's 27</strong>—and record your scores once every seven days. Use our <a href="/tools/average-calculator/">Interactive Average Calculator</a> to monitor your three-dart average trends across weeks. If your 100-dart score rises by 15 points over a month, your match scoring average will inevitably climb alongside it.</p>
          </div>
      </section>

